{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"`SupportsIncremental` (`_engine/rebar_reconciler/_backend.py:529-536`) is a declared capability with\nzero implementers and zero dispatch. Its docstring asserts, in the present tense, a behaviour that\ndoes not exist:\n\n> Core uses an incremental fetch only when ``isinstance(backend, SupportsIncremental)``; otherwise\n> it falls back to a full scan.\n\nNo adapter defines `search_incremental`, and no `isinstance(backend, SupportsIncremental)` check\nexists anywhere in `src/`. ADR 0035:192 lists it beside `SupportsLinks` and `SupportsComments` — both\nimplemented by Cloud and DC — with no marker distinguishing it.\n\n## Origin (do not re-derive)\n\nStory `ce4d-c3ca-5412-4798` (closed) introduced it while landing the Backend port with \"zero\nbehavior change\", deferring all wiring to S4 `6673-7636-a116-4f90`. S4 closed without mentioning\nincremental. ce4d's deliverable was the *capability-detection mechanism* — `isinstance` against\n`@runtime_checkable` Protocols, explicitly \"no `capabilities()` method\" — with `FakeBackend`\nexercising \"the guarded path by inheriting the opt-in Protocol and the skip path by omitting it\".\n`SupportsIncremental` has been serving as that skip path. `FakeBackend` is test-only, so wiring real\nbackends does NOT invalidate `test_backends_without_incremental_are_detected`.\n\n## The capability is real, and opt-in is the right modelling\n\nVerified against vendor documentation: Jira exposes JQL `updated > \"...\"` via\n`/rest/api/3/search/jql`; Linear exposes `issues(filter: { updatedAt: { gt: ... } })`; GitHub Issues\nexposes `since=` on `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues`. All three allow sorting by the field they\nfilter on, so resumption works.\n\nOpt-in stays correct for a stronger reason than \"a backend might lack it\": **no tracker's incremental\nquery can report deletions.** Jira and GitHub cannot at all; Linear sees archival but not hard\ndeletion. A correct caller must therefore run a periodic full scan regardless, to detect absence by\nset difference. Incremental is a bandwidth optimization that sits ALONGSIDE the full scan, never\nreplacing it.\n\n## The correctness hazard — this is the crux\n\n`fetch_snapshot` / `_build_snapshot` (`fetcher.py:734`) produces a snapshot that is a COMPLETE\npicture. ADR 0028 §1-2 is load-bearing: \"Snapshot-absence is NOT a signal of deletion... Deletion is\nproven only by a bounded direct GET returning 404.\" A snapshot built from an incremental delta\ncontains only changed items — feeding that to any absence-sensitive path would read as mass\ndeletion.\n\nCompounding, on Jira specifically:\n\n- Atlassian documents the search index lagging writes by \"a few seconds to minutes\"\n  (Search and Reconcile). This repo already knows this: `_backend.py:267`'s `get_issue_by_rest`\n  exists to \"Read an issue from the primary store, bypassing any search-index lag\".\n- Search truncates `updated` to whole seconds while `GET /issue` returns milliseconds — so\n  `max(row.updated)` is a COARSER value than the entity's real timestamp and is unsafe as the next\n  watermark without an overlap margin.\n- JRACLOUD-66284 (Closed / **Won't Fix**): remote-link add/remove bumps the displayed `updated` but\n  the issue does NOT appear in an `updated`-filtered search. Comments DO bump it.\n\n## Signature defects to fix while implementing\n\n1. **`since: str` is a false least-common-denominator.** Verified against live Jira Cloud: JQL\n   rejects `'2026-08-06T20:00:00Z'`, `'...+00:00'`, and `'2026-08-06 20:00:00'`; only\n   `'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm'` (minute granularity, evaluated in the ACCOUNT's timezone) or unquoted\n   epoch-millis parse. Linear and GitHub take ISO-8601. Type the watermark as an aware `datetime` and\n   let each adapter format it.\n2. **The return type must carry the next watermark**, not just rows. Given the truncation and index\n   lag above, the safe next watermark is not derivable by the caller from the rows alone; the\n   tracker-specific overlap margin belongs in the adapter.\n3. **The name over-promises.** The guarantee is \"narrows a scan by an update watermark, missing\n   deletions, possibly missing sub-entity mutations, possibly missing writes still inside the\n   indexing window\" — consider `SupportsUpdatedSinceFilter`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `search_incremental` is implemented on BOTH Jira providers: `adapters/jira/` (Cloud) and\n      `adapters/jira_datacenter/`\n- [ ] Core dispatches through an `isinstance(backend, SupportsIncremental)` guard with a full-scan\n      fallback, so the docstring's claim becomes true\n- [ ] The watermark parameter is not a bare `str`; the Jira adapters convert to a JQL-parseable form\n      and a round-trip test proves an ISO-8601 input does not reach JQL\n- [ ] The capability returns the watermark to resume from, with a documented per-adapter overlap\n      margin covering Jira's second-truncation and index lag\n- [ ] **No absence-sensitive path can observe an incremental result as a complete snapshot.**\n      MECHANISM (chosen — plan-review G6): the delta is MERGED ONTO THE PRIOR PERSISTED FULL\n      SNAPSHOT before use; incremental output is never handed to the differ as a standalone\n      snapshot. Data-flow point: the incremental result enters at snapshot assembly in\n      `fetcher.py` `_build_snapshot` — changed items overlay the previously persisted snapshot by\n      key (upsert), and only the MERGED, complete snapshot is written and diffed. The differ\n      continues to treat the snapshot as authoritative and absence-sensitive (`fetcher.py:513-515`);\n      because the merged snapshot is a complete picture, ADR 0028's \"absence is not deletion\"\n      invariant does not regress. A test pins that an incremental delta on its own is never\n      observed by the differ as a complete snapshot (the merge is mandatory), and that ADR 0028's\n      invariant holds on the merged result\n- [ ] The periodic full scan is retained and its cadence documented; incremental never replaces it\n- [ ] `test_backends_without_incremental_are_detected` still passes (FakeBackend stays the negative\n      case), and a new test asserts both Jira backends now satisfy the capability\n- [ ] The docstring states the real guarantee and its known blind spots, citing JRACLOUD-66284\n- [ ] ADR 0035:192 is amended via the established `> **AMENDED by [rebar:<id>]**` convention\n- [ ] Measured (scoped — plan-review G6): for the entity classes incremental can actually\n      observe — creations and field-updates of NON-DELETED issues whose `updated` timestamp has\n      cleared the adapter's overlap-margin/indexing window — a pass using incremental fetch\n      (merged per AC5) returns the SAME reconciliation decisions as a full-scan pass over the same\n      window. Deletions and remote-link-only mutations (JRACLOUD-66284) are EXPLICITLY EXCLUDED\n      from this equivalence — they are unobservable to any incremental query and remain the\n      responsibility of the retained periodic full scan (the \"full scan retained\" AC above); the\n      measured test asserts equivalence only over these observable classes, consistent with the\n      plan's documented blind spots"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786126083967432001,"uuid":"01c64cd8-d741-4635-869b-8379b3b16e4d"}